{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start is built on top of TanStack Router, so all of the features of TanStack Router are available to you."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We highly recommend reading the "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/framework/react/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to learn more about the features and capabilities of TanStack Router. What you learn here is more of a high-level overview of TanStack Router and how it works in Start."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-router","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Router"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" file is the file that will dictate the behavior of TanStack Router used within Start. It's located in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src"},{"type":"text","value":" directory of your project."}]},{"type":"code","value":"src/\n├── router.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, you can configure everything from the default "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/preloading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"preloading functionality"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/data-loading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"caching staleness"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/router.tsx\nimport { createRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { routeTree } from './routeTree.gen'\n\n// You must export a getRouter function that\n// returns a new router instance each time\nexport function getRouter() {\n  const router = createRouter({\n    routeTree,\n    scrollRestoration: true,\n  })\n\n  return router\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"file-based-routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File-Based Routing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start uses TanStack Router's file-based routing approach to ensure proper code-splitting and advanced type-safety."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can find your routes in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/routes"},{"type":"text","value":" directory."}]},{"type":"code","value":"src/\n├── routes <-- This is where you put your routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── about.tsx\n│   ├── posts.tsx\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-root-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Root Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The root route is the top-most route in the entire tree and encapsulates all other routes as children. It's found in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/routes/__root.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" file and must be named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__root.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"src/\n├── routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx <-- The root route"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It has no path and is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"always"}]},{"type":"text","value":" matched"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"component"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"always"}]},{"type":"text","value":" rendered"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is where you render your document shell, e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<html>"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<body>"},{"type":"text","value":", etc."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because it is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"always rendered"}]},{"type":"text","value":", it is the perfect place to construct your application shell and take care of any global logic"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/__root.tsx\nimport {\n  Outlet,\n  createRootRoute,\n  HeadContent,\n  Scripts,\n} from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport type { ReactNode } from 'react'\n\nexport const Route = createRootRoute({\n  head: () => ({\n    meta: [\n      {\n        charSet: 'utf-8',\n      },\n      {\n        name: 'viewport',\n        content: 'width=device-width, initial-scale=1',\n      },\n      {\n        title: 'TanStack Start Starter',\n      },\n    ],\n  }),\n  component: RootComponent,\n})\n\nfunction RootComponent() {\n  return (\n    <RootDocument>\n      <Outlet />\n    </RootDocument>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction RootDocument({ children }: Readonly<{ children: ReactNode }>) {\n  return (\n    <html>\n      <head>\n        <HeadContent />\n      </head>\n      <body>\n        {children}\n        <Scripts />\n      </body>\n    </html>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Notice the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scripts"},{"type":"text","value":" component at the bottom of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<body>"},{"type":"text","value":" tag. This is used to load all of the client-side JavaScript for the application and should always be included for proper functionality."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-headcontent-component","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The HeadContent Component"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HeadContent"},{"type":"text","value":" component is used to render the head, title, meta, link, and head-related script tags of the document."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It should be "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rendered in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<head>"},{"type":"text","value":" tag of your root route's layout."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-outlet-component","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Outlet Component"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Outlet"},{"type":"text","value":" component is used to render the next potentially matching child route. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Outlet />"},{"type":"text","value":" doesn't take any props and can be rendered anywhere within a route's component tree. If there is no matching child route, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Outlet />"},{"type":"text","value":" will render "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-scripts-component","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Scripts Component"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scripts"},{"type":"text","value":" component is used to render the body scripts of the document."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It should be "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rendered in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<body>"},{"type":"text","value":" tag of your root route's layout."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"route-tree-generation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Route Tree Generation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may notice a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routeTree.gen.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file in your project."}]},{"type":"code","value":"src/\n├── routeTree.gen.ts <-- The generated route tree file"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This file is automatically generated when you run TanStack Start (via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"npm run dev"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"npm run start"},{"type":"text","value":"). This file contains the generated route tree and a handful of TS utilities that make TanStack Start's type-safety extremely fast and fully inferred."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"nested-routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested Routing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router uses nested routing to match the URL with the correct component tree to render."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, given the following routes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"routes/\n├── __root.tsx <-- Renders the <Root> component\n├── posts.tsx <-- Renders the <Posts> component\n├── posts.$postId.tsx <-- Renders the <Post> component"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And the URL: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/123"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The component tree would look like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Root>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post />\n  </Posts>\n</Root>"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"types-of-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types of Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a few different types of routes that you can create in your project."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Index Routes - Matched when the URL is exactly the same as the route's path"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic/Wildcard/Splat Routes - Dynamically capture part or all of the URL path into a variable to use in your application"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are also a few different utility route types that you can use to group and organize your routes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pathless Layout Routes (Apply layout or logic to a group of routes without nesting them in a path)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-Nested Routes (Un-nest a route from its parents and render its own component tree)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped Routes (Group routes together in a directory simply for organization, without affecting the path hierarchy)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"route-tree-configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Route Tree Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The route tree is configured in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/routes"},{"type":"text","value":" directory."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-file-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating File Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To create a route, create a new file that corresponds to the path of the route you want to create. For example:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Path"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filename"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Index Route"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/about"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"about.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static Route"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Layout\" Route"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts/index.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Index Route"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/:postId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts/$postId.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Route"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/rest/*"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rest/$.tsx"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wildcard Route"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defining-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To define a route, use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFileRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" function to export the route as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Route"},{"type":"text","value":" variable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, to handle the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/:postId"},{"type":"text","value":" route, you would create a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"posts/$postId.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" here:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"src/\n├── routes\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then, define the route like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/posts/$postId.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/posts/$postId')({\n  component: PostComponent,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The path string passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFileRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"automatically written and managed by the router for you via the TanStack Router Bundler Plugin or Router CLI."}]},{"type":"text","value":" So, as you create new routes, move routes around or rename routes, the path will be updated for you automatically."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"this-is-just-the-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is just the \"start\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This has been just a high-level overview of how to configure routes using TanStack Router. For more detailed information, please refer to the "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-router","text":"The Router","level":2},{"id":"file-based-routing","text":"File-Based Routing","level":2},{"id":"the-root-route","text":"The Root Route","level":2},{"id":"the-headcontent-component","text":"The HeadContent Component","level":2},{"id":"the-outlet-component","text":"The Outlet Component","level":2},{"id":"the-scripts-component","text":"The Scripts Component","level":2},{"id":"route-tree-generation","text":"Route Tree Generation","level":2},{"id":"nested-routing","text":"Nested Routing","level":2},{"id":"types-of-routes","text":"Types of Routes","level":2},{"id":"route-tree-configuration","text":"Route Tree Configuration","level":2},{"id":"creating-file-routes","text":"Creating File Routes","level":2},{"id":"defining-routes","text":"Defining Routes","level":2},{"id":"this-is-just-the-start","text":"This is just the \"start\"","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Start is built on top of TanStack Router, so all of the features of TanStack Router are available to you.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> We highly recommend reading the [TanStack Router documentation](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/overview) to learn more about the features and capabilities of TanStack Router. What you learn here is more of a high-level overview of TanStack Router and how it works in Start.\n\n## The Router\n\nThe `router.tsx` file is the file that will dictate the behavior of TanStack Router used within Start. It's located in the `src` directory of your project.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── router.tsx\n```\n\nHere, you can configure everything from the default [preloading functionality](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/preloading) to [caching staleness](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/data-loading).\n\n```tsx\n// src/router.tsx\nimport { createRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { routeTree } from './routeTree.gen'\n\n// You must export a getRouter function that\n// returns a new router instance each time\nexport function getRouter() {\n  const router = createRouter({\n    routeTree,\n    scrollRestoration: true,\n  })\n\n  return router\n}\n```\n\n## File-Based Routing\n\nStart uses TanStack Router's file-based routing approach to ensure proper code-splitting and advanced type-safety.\n\nYou can find your routes in the `src/routes` directory.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes <-- This is where you put your routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── about.tsx\n│   ├── posts.tsx\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx\n```\n\n## The Root Route\n\nThe root route is the top-most route in the entire tree and encapsulates all other routes as children. It's found in the `src/routes/__root.tsx` file and must be named `__root.tsx`.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx <-- The root route\n```\n\n- It has no path and is **always** matched\n- Its `component` is **always** rendered\n- This is where you render your document shell, e.g. `<html>`, `<body>`, etc.\n- Because it is **always rendered**, it is the perfect place to construct your application shell and take care of any global logic\n\n```tsx\n// src/routes/__root.tsx\nimport {\n  Outlet,\n  createRootRoute,\n  HeadContent,\n  Scripts,\n} from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport type { ReactNode } from 'react'\n\nexport const Route = createRootRoute({\n  head: () => ({\n    meta: [\n      {\n        charSet: 'utf-8',\n      },\n      {\n        name: 'viewport',\n        content: 'width=device-width, initial-scale=1',\n      },\n      {\n        title: 'TanStack Start Starter',\n      },\n    ],\n  }),\n  component: RootComponent,\n})\n\nfunction RootComponent() {\n  return (\n    <RootDocument>\n      <Outlet />\n    </RootDocument>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction RootDocument({ children }: Readonly<{ children: ReactNode }>) {\n  return (\n    <html>\n      <head>\n        <HeadContent />\n      </head>\n      <body>\n        {children}\n        <Scripts />\n      </body>\n    </html>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nNotice the `Scripts` component at the bottom of the `<body>` tag. This is used to load all of the client-side JavaScript for the application and should always be included for proper functionality.\n\n## The HeadContent Component\n\nThe `HeadContent` component is used to render the head, title, meta, link, and head-related script tags of the document.\n\nIt should be **rendered in the `<head>` tag of your root route's layout.**\n\n## The Outlet Component\n\nThe `Outlet` component is used to render the next potentially matching child route. `<Outlet />` doesn't take any props and can be rendered anywhere within a route's component tree. If there is no matching child route, `<Outlet />` will render `null`.\n\n## The Scripts Component\n\nThe `Scripts` component is used to render the body scripts of the document.\n\nIt should be **rendered in the `<body>` tag of your root route's layout.**\n\n## Route Tree Generation\n\nYou may notice a `routeTree.gen.ts` file in your project.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routeTree.gen.ts <-- The generated route tree file\n```\n\nThis file is automatically generated when you run TanStack Start (via `npm run dev` or `npm run start`). This file contains the generated route tree and a handful of TS utilities that make TanStack Start's type-safety extremely fast and fully inferred.\n\n## Nested Routing\n\nTanStack Router uses nested routing to match the URL with the correct component tree to render.\n\nFor example, given the following routes:\n\n```\nroutes/\n├── __root.tsx <-- Renders the <Root> component\n├── posts.tsx <-- Renders the <Posts> component\n├── posts.$postId.tsx <-- Renders the <Post> component\n```\n\nAnd the URL: `/posts/123`\n\nThe component tree would look like this:\n\n```\n<Root>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post />\n  </Posts>\n</Root>\n```\n\n## Types of Routes\n\nThere are a few different types of routes that you can create in your project.\n\n- Index Routes - Matched when the URL is exactly the same as the route's path\n- Dynamic/Wildcard/Splat Routes - Dynamically capture part or all of the URL path into a variable to use in your application\n\nThere are also a few different utility route types that you can use to group and organize your routes\n\n- Pathless Layout Routes (Apply layout or logic to a group of routes without nesting them in a path)\n- Non-Nested Routes (Un-nest a route from its parents and render its own component tree)\n- Grouped Routes (Group routes together in a directory simply for organization, without affecting the path hierarchy)\n\n## Route Tree Configuration\n\nThe route tree is configured in the `src/routes` directory.\n\n## Creating File Routes\n\nTo create a route, create a new file that corresponds to the path of the route you want to create. For example:\n\n| Path             | Filename            | Type           |\n| ---------------- | ------------------- | -------------- |\n| `/`              | `index.tsx`         | Index Route    |\n| `/about`         | `about.tsx`         | Static Route   |\n|                  | `posts.tsx`         | \"Layout\" Route |\n| `/posts/`        | `posts/index.tsx`   | Index Route    |\n| `/posts/:postId` | `posts/$postId.tsx` | Dynamic Route  |\n| `/rest/*`        | `rest/$.tsx`        | Wildcard Route |\n\n## Defining Routes\n\nTo define a route, use the `createFileRoute` function to export the route as the `Route` variable.\n\nFor example, to handle the `/posts/:postId` route, you would create a file named `posts/$postId.tsx` here:\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx\n```\n\nThen, define the route like this:\n\n```tsx\n// src/routes/posts/$postId.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/posts/$postId')({\n  component: PostComponent,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The path string passed to `createFileRoute` is **automatically written and managed by the router for you via the TanStack Router Bundler Plugin or Router CLI.** So, as you create new routes, move routes around or rename routes, the path will be updated for you automatically.\n\n## This is just the \"start\"\n\nThis has been just a high-level overview of how to configure routes using TanStack Router. For more detailed information, please refer to the [TanStack Router documentation](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: routing\ntitle: Routing\n---\n\nTanStack Start is built on top of TanStack Router, so all of the features of TanStack Router are available to you.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> We highly recommend reading the [TanStack Router documentation](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/overview) to learn more about the features and capabilities of TanStack Router. What you learn here is more of a high-level overview of TanStack Router and how it works in Start.\n\n## The Router\n\nThe `router.tsx` file is the file that will dictate the behavior of TanStack Router used within Start. It's located in the `src` directory of your project.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── router.tsx\n```\n\nHere, you can configure everything from the default [preloading functionality](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/preloading) to [caching staleness](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/data-loading).\n\n```tsx\n// src/router.tsx\nimport { createRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { routeTree } from './routeTree.gen'\n\n// You must export a getRouter function that\n// returns a new router instance each time\nexport function getRouter() {\n  const router = createRouter({\n    routeTree,\n    scrollRestoration: true,\n  })\n\n  return router\n}\n```\n\n## File-Based Routing\n\nStart uses TanStack Router's file-based routing approach to ensure proper code-splitting and advanced type-safety.\n\nYou can find your routes in the `src/routes` directory.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes <-- This is where you put your routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── about.tsx\n│   ├── posts.tsx\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx\n```\n\n## The Root Route\n\nThe root route is the top-most route in the entire tree and encapsulates all other routes as children. It's found in the `src/routes/__root.tsx` file and must be named `__root.tsx`.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes\n│   ├── __root.tsx <-- The root route\n```\n\n- It has no path and is **always** matched\n- Its `component` is **always** rendered\n- This is where you render your document shell, e.g. `<html>`, `<body>`, etc.\n- Because it is **always rendered**, it is the perfect place to construct your application shell and take care of any global logic\n\n```tsx\n// src/routes/__root.tsx\nimport {\n  Outlet,\n  createRootRoute,\n  HeadContent,\n  Scripts,\n} from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport type { ReactNode } from 'react'\n\nexport const Route = createRootRoute({\n  head: () => ({\n    meta: [\n      {\n        charSet: 'utf-8',\n      },\n      {\n        name: 'viewport',\n        content: 'width=device-width, initial-scale=1',\n      },\n      {\n        title: 'TanStack Start Starter',\n      },\n    ],\n  }),\n  component: RootComponent,\n})\n\nfunction RootComponent() {\n  return (\n    <RootDocument>\n      <Outlet />\n    </RootDocument>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction RootDocument({ children }: Readonly<{ children: ReactNode }>) {\n  return (\n    <html>\n      <head>\n        <HeadContent />\n      </head>\n      <body>\n        {children}\n        <Scripts />\n      </body>\n    </html>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nNotice the `Scripts` component at the bottom of the `<body>` tag. This is used to load all of the client-side JavaScript for the application and should always be included for proper functionality.\n\n## The HeadContent Component\n\nThe `HeadContent` component is used to render the head, title, meta, link, and head-related script tags of the document.\n\nIt should be **rendered in the `<head>` tag of your root route's layout.**\n\n## The Outlet Component\n\nThe `Outlet` component is used to render the next potentially matching child route. `<Outlet />` doesn't take any props and can be rendered anywhere within a route's component tree. If there is no matching child route, `<Outlet />` will render `null`.\n\n## The Scripts Component\n\nThe `Scripts` component is used to render the body scripts of the document.\n\nIt should be **rendered in the `<body>` tag of your root route's layout.**\n\n## Route Tree Generation\n\nYou may notice a `routeTree.gen.ts` file in your project.\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routeTree.gen.ts <-- The generated route tree file\n```\n\nThis file is automatically generated when you run TanStack Start (via `npm run dev` or `npm run start`). This file contains the generated route tree and a handful of TS utilities that make TanStack Start's type-safety extremely fast and fully inferred.\n\n## Nested Routing\n\nTanStack Router uses nested routing to match the URL with the correct component tree to render.\n\nFor example, given the following routes:\n\n```\nroutes/\n├── __root.tsx <-- Renders the <Root> component\n├── posts.tsx <-- Renders the <Posts> component\n├── posts.$postId.tsx <-- Renders the <Post> component\n```\n\nAnd the URL: `/posts/123`\n\nThe component tree would look like this:\n\n```\n<Root>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post />\n  </Posts>\n</Root>\n```\n\n## Types of Routes\n\nThere are a few different types of routes that you can create in your project.\n\n- Index Routes - Matched when the URL is exactly the same as the route's path\n- Dynamic/Wildcard/Splat Routes - Dynamically capture part or all of the URL path into a variable to use in your application\n\nThere are also a few different utility route types that you can use to group and organize your routes\n\n- Pathless Layout Routes (Apply layout or logic to a group of routes without nesting them in a path)\n- Non-Nested Routes (Un-nest a route from its parents and render its own component tree)\n- Grouped Routes (Group routes together in a directory simply for organization, without affecting the path hierarchy)\n\n## Route Tree Configuration\n\nThe route tree is configured in the `src/routes` directory.\n\n## Creating File Routes\n\nTo create a route, create a new file that corresponds to the path of the route you want to create. For example:\n\n| Path             | Filename            | Type           |\n| ---------------- | ------------------- | -------------- |\n| `/`              | `index.tsx`         | Index Route    |\n| `/about`         | `about.tsx`         | Static Route   |\n|                  | `posts.tsx`         | \"Layout\" Route |\n| `/posts/`        | `posts/index.tsx`   | Index Route    |\n| `/posts/:postId` | `posts/$postId.tsx` | Dynamic Route  |\n| `/rest/*`        | `rest/$.tsx`        | Wildcard Route |\n\n## Defining Routes\n\nTo define a route, use the `createFileRoute` function to export the route as the `Route` variable.\n\nFor example, to handle the `/posts/:postId` route, you would create a file named `posts/$postId.tsx` here:\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── routes\n│   ├── posts/$postId.tsx\n```\n\nThen, define the route like this:\n\n```tsx\n// src/routes/posts/$postId.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/posts/$postId')({\n  component: PostComponent,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The path string passed to `createFileRoute` is **automatically written and managed by the router for you via the TanStack Router Bundler Plugin or Router CLI.** So, as you create new routes, move routes around or rename routes, the path will be updated for you automatically.\n\n## This is just the \"start\"\n\nThis has been just a high-level overview of how to configure routes using TanStack Router. For more detailed information, please refer to the [TanStack Router documentation](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing).\n"}
